 ## Overview
-Deferred Voxel Shading is a real-time global illumination technique that merges voxel cone tracing with deferred shading to approximate indirect lighting effects such as diffuse/specular reflection, color blending, emissive materials, and shadows [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of computing multiple light bounces and complex lighting interactions in scenes with many light sources by delaying lighting calculations until the fragment shader stage, thereby avoiding redundant computations for overlapping geometry [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard voxel cone tracing which may operate in forward rendering, this method explicitly leverages a deferred rendering pipeline to enhance performance in high-light-count scenarios [S1, unofficial].
+Deferred Voxel Shading is a real-time global illumination technique that merges voxel cone tracing with deferred shading to compute indirect lighting effects such as diffuse/specular reflection, color blending, emissive materials, and ambient occlusion [S2, unofficial]. Unlike forward rendering approaches, it delays lighting computations until after all geometry is rendered, avoiding redundant calculations for overlapping polygons and enhancing performance in scenes with many light sources [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Voxel Cone Tracing](1454-voxel-cone-tracing.md) by introducing anisotropic voxel filtering and a dedicated voxel illumination pass [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method consists of four distinct stages: Voxelization, Voxel Illumination, Anisotropic Voxel Generation, and Voxel Cone Tracing [S2, unofficial].
+The method consists of four main stages: Voxelization, Voxel Illumination, Anisotropic Voxel Generation, and Voxel Cone Tracing [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs:** Scene geometry (vertices, normals, albedo, emission), light sources (point, spot, directional), camera parameters, and material properties (albedo, specular exponent).
-*   **Outputs:** Final screen-space color with indirect illumination, shadows, and ambient occlusion.
+**Inputs:**
+*   Scene geometry (triangles).
+*   Material properties: Albedo ($\rho$), Surface Normal ($N$), Emission ($E$), Specular exponent.
+*   Light sources: Point, Spot, Directional.
+*   Camera parameters: Position, View Direction, Aperture angle ($\theta$).
+
+**Outputs:**
+*   Final fragment color with indirect illumination.
 
